# Every text file is LF in the repository and LF in the working tree.
#
# Added 2026-09-03 while closing Iteration 5. `core.autocrlf` is `true` on this
# machine, so git converted on checkout and `git diff` warned on all 21 touched
# files. Iteration 5 met the same problem from the other direction: scripted
# edits reintroduced CRLF into four source files, and every later edit had to
# normalise by hand before it could be trusted.
#
# `text=auto` still detects binary content and leaves it alone; `eol=lf` pins
# the working-tree ending regardless of each developer's `core.autocrlf`, which
# is what makes this a property of the repository rather than of a machine.
#
# Safe to introduce here: the index already stored LF for all 71 text files, so
# this normalises nothing and produces no renormalisation diff.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Redundant under the rule above, and kept anyway for the reason attached to
# them: Linux containers execute these, and a CRLF line ending breaks the
# shebang and produces a confusing "no such file or directory" at container
# init. The rule can be re-derived; the reason cannot.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.sql text eol=lf
Dockerfile text eol=lf
